  "name": "Question 19",
  "problem": "A particle moves with a constant acceleration of $(3i+2j)\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ The velocity of the particle is $(-7i-6j)\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s}.$ Find the time when the velocity of the particle equals $2i\\,\\text{m/s}.$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that when a particle moves with constant acceleration, the velocity vector at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nv=u+at,\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is the initial velocity, and $a$ is the acceleration of the particle.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\na=3i+2j,\\,u=-7i-6j,\\,v=2i\n$$\n\ninto the equation above, we can solve for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =u+at \\\\ 2i & =(-7i-6j)+(3i+2j)t \\\\ 9i+6j & =(3i+2j)t \\\\ 9i+6j & =3ti+2tj\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the $i$ and $j$ components in the last equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}9=3t \\\\ 6=2t\\end{aligned}\\,\u27f9\\,t=3\\,\\text{s}.\n$$",
